`std::lock_guard`'s constructor acquires a mutex lock, and its destructor automatically releases the lock when the `lock_guard` object goes out of scope. This prevents deadlocks or forgotten unlocks, even if an exception is thrown between the lock and unlock operations.
```cpp
std::mutex m;
void func() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m); // Lock acquired
    // ... critical section ...
} // Lock released automatically when 'lock' goes out of scope
```

Without RAII, you would need explicit `try-catch` blocks or careful `goto` statements to ensure `delete` is called in all possible exit paths. This leads to verbose, error-prone code with duplicated resource release logic.
```cpp
int* p = nullptr;
try {
    p = new int(10);
    // ... potentially throwing operations ...
    delete p; // Must be reached
} catch (...) {
    delete p; // Must be repeated here
    throw;
}
```
